trace.content:
[THOUGHT] LDH is elevated — a nonspecific but highly relevant finding in lymphoma, as well as in other malignancies, hemolysis, or tissue injury. In the context of B symptoms (night sweats), weight loss, abdominal distension, and pain in a 55-year-old male, elevated LDH strengthens suspicion for lymphoma — particularly if accompanied by lymphadenopathy or organomegaly. However, LDH alone is insufficient for diagnosis. The abdominal ultrasound remains the most direct way to identify ascites, peritoneal thickening/nodules, mesenteric lymphadenopathy, or splenomegaly — all common in lymphomatous peritonitis or abdominal lymphoma. Since the ultrasound was requested earlier (Turn 3) and not yet provided, and given that 11 turns have been used with critical imaging still pending, I must now explicitly retrieve that result — it is the single highest-yield structural assessment available and essential to differentiate ascites etiology (malignant vs. portal hypertensive vs. infectious). I will request it *once more*, phrasing it as a retrieval of the finalized report, as this is justified by clinical urgency and absence of prior receipt.

[TEST] Please provide the finalized abdominal ultrasound report, including:  
- Presence, location, and estimated volume of ascites;  
- Size and echotexture of liver and spleen;  
- Presence of peritoneal thickening, nodularity, or plaques;  
- Mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy (size, morphology);  
- Any focal masses or organ infiltration.

